 and optimizer.py. All are new files, so the base monolith text is not in this payload. I judged behavior against the binding contract the candidate tree actually ships (_audit_emit_parts/__init__.py:20-48 install() and the facade _lib/audit_emit.py:413-480). No tool here executes code, so every check below was done by reading and grep.\\n\\nHOW I CHECKED THE MOVED CODE (WHAT TO VERIFY item 4)\\n- Name lookup: install() rebinds each exported function to the facade's globals with FunctionType(code, facade_ns, ...), so a lookup or monkeypatch of _write_event, _scrub_ceo_boot_event, _breadcrumb and the rest lands on _lib.audit_emit. I cross-checked every free name used in the five dispatch groups and in harness, lifecycle and operations against the __all__ of the 33 installed parts and the facade's own definitions (_KNOWN_ACTIONS, _GENERIC_DISPATCH_HANDLERS, emit_generic, the locks at audit_emit.py:483-484). Every name resolves. The _OPTIMIZER_ALLOWLISTS keys used in group 01 exist in boot_schemas.py:206-230. There is no latent NameError or KeyError on a dispatch path.\\n- No duplicate definitions: none of the constants defined in this payload's files is also defined in another part, so declarations.update() cannot silently shadow one.\\n- Rebinding covers every function: the parts contain no decorators, classes or lambdas, so nothing escapes install() with template globals.\\n- Parts import on their own: module-level code uses only re (imported in harness_schemas.py:7 and lifecycle_schemas.py:7) and two relative imports (harness_schemas.py:8 and optimizer.py:7), and those resolve to tuples or frozensets. Annotations such as Dict, Any, List and Optional sit under `from __future__ import annotations` or on locals, so they are never evaluated.\\n- Copying is safe: deepcopy of the frozensets, dicts and compiled patterns is valid on Python 3.7+ (re.Pattern deepcopy returns self), and no Lock or module object appears in any __all__.\\n- Dispatch is complete: 173 `if action ==/in` branches, 173 `state[\\\"event\\\"] = event` lines and 173 `return True`, so no branch can write the unscrubbed event. The only three bare returns are the intended closed-enum drops: persona_coverage_synthesized, tier_policy_loader_fallback_observed and audit_producer_path_pollution_detected. _dispatch_generic treats None as drop, True as write and False as try the next group. No action name has a branch in two groups. The passthrough check is last (generic_dispatch_05.py:352), and default-deny still writes {\\\"action\\\"} only (generic_dispatch_05.py:375-377).\\n- The ghost-action guard follows the new layout: test_audit_emit_ghost_action_guard.py:59-76 parses _GENERIC_DISPATCH_HANDLERS.\\n- Python 3.9 and stdlib-only: met.\\n\\nDECLARED CONDITIONS THIS PAYLOAD TOUCHES\\n- Condition 5: true. check_arbitration_kernel.py:312 inscribes \\\".claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/**/*\\\", which covers every file in this payload.\\n- Condition 7: true. Logic moved to modules, the facade keeps the path, and a hand patch to the old monolith would have to be redone in the part.\\n- Condition 6: consistent here. lifecycle_schemas.py:508 adds the \\\"bash_indirect\\\" rail and the CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD and CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS switches, which match the producer at bash_indirect_telemetry.py:59-60. A disabled-rail event is therefore not coerced to \\\"observe\\\" or \\\"other\\\".\\n- No other condition is affected by these files. I found no inversion of fail direction, no write outside its place and no partial write in these files: they only declare data and call _write_event.\\n\\nP0: none.\\nP1: none.\\n\\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): none.\\n\\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\\nP2-1. _lib/audit_emit.py:473-480 (the facade, which ships in another payload but is this code's only entry point). emit_generic carries two do-nothing branches, `if action == \\\"tool_call_lifecycle_recorded\\\"` and `if action == \\\"settings_tamper_detected\\\"`. Both just call _dispatch_generic. They keep test_ceo_boot_tamper_tripwires.py:525 green, because that test greps inspect.getsource(emit_generic) for the branch. The real scrub gates are generic_dispatch_04.py:62 and :159. Scenario: delete the real settings_tamper_detected branch and that source assertion still passes. The ghost-action guard would still go red, so the cover is thinner, not gone. Minimal fix: point the assertion at the sources in _GENERIC_DISPATCH_HANDLERS, as the ghost-action guard does, and delete the stub branches.\\n\\nP2-2. The part modules only work through install(). Example: harness_schemas.py:12-14 _learning_token_field uses _LEARNING_ID_RE, which lives in lifecycle_schemas and is never imported. Every emitter in harness.py, lifecycle.py and operations.py likewise uses _write_event and _scrub_ceo_boot_event, which they don't define. Scenario: a future caller does `from _lib._audit_emit_parts.harness import emit_codex_tool_recorded` and gets a NameError at call time instead of a fail-open emit. Today the only importers are install() (audit_emit.py:413, check_agent_spawn.py:128, scripts/lessons.py:204) and audit_emit.py:21. The first iter_events (audit_emit.py:382) delegates to the unbound replay template, but it is replaced before any caller can reach it. Minimal fix: a test that rejects any import of a `_lib._audit_emit_parts.<part>` outside the facade binders, and drop the line-21 import.\\n\\nP2-3. The failure unit grew. `import _lib.audit_emit` now needs all 33 parts plus a deepcopy of every declaration at each hook start, so one missing or unreadable part (an interrupted copy or a partial upgrade) breaks every emitter at once. Before, the failure unit was one file. install.sh:1522-1541 copies `_lib/*` entry by entry with cp -R. I could not check upgrade.sh: it has no redacted copy in this tree (no scripts/upgrade.sh), presumably among the withheld files. Through _python-hook.sh a crash with no decision fails open, which fits the infra-fault rule. For the fail-CLOSED canonical-edit wall (ADR-186), the effect depends on that hook's own import guard, which is in another payload. Minimal fix: an install and upgrade smoke step that imports _lib.audit_emit in the target tree after the copy.\\n\\nP2-4. generic_dispatch_04.py:266. The redactor replaced the clamp ceiling of compaction_continuity_snapshot.chain_length with [REDACTED:rg], so this payload cannot show whether the value equals the base. This is not a defect claim. Minimal fix: the maintainer confirms the literal against the v1.4.3-rc.1 monolith.\\n\\nP2-5. The dispatch bodies were regenerated mechanically. Signs: redundant parentheses such as `(_hs > 599)` and `\\\"\\\".join((c for c ...))`, and a leftover `pass` at generic_dispatch_05.py:353. The per-branch security-rationale comments of the old emit_generic did not survive into the groups. Behavior is unaffected. Minimal fix (optional): restore the rationale comments or point to them in ADR-202.\",\"decision\":\"GO\",\"sentence\":\"The thirteen new audit_emit part modules are a complete, consistently rebound split: every free name resolves through install() into the facade, all 173 dispatch branches scrub and set the event before writing, the closed-enum drops and default-deny are preserved, and the conditions this payload touches (5, 7 and the condition-6 enum) hold against the code, so it leaves no false condition and no P0, only P2 follow-ups.\"}","structured_output":{"report":"SCOPE OF PAYLOAD 2/32: thirteen new files under .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/ that the PLAN-197 split of _lib/audit_emit.py produced: generic_dispatch_01..05.py, governance.py, harness.py, harness_schemas.py, lifecycle.py, lifecycle_schemas.py, operations.py and optimizer.py. All are new files, so the base monolith text is not in this payload. I judged behavior against the binding contract the candidate tree actually ships (_audit_emit_parts/__init__.py:20-48 install() and the facade _lib/audit_emit.py:413-480). No tool here executes code, so every check below was done by reading and grep.\n\nHOW I CHECKED THE MOVED CODE (WHAT TO VERIFY item 4)\n- Name lookup: install() rebinds each exported function to the facade's globals with FunctionType(code, facade_ns, ...), so a lookup or monkeypatch of _write_event, _scrub_ceo_boot_event, _breadcrumb and the rest lands on _lib.audit_emit. I cross-checked every free name used in the five dispatch groups and in harness, lifecycle and operations against the __all__ of the 33 installed parts and the facade's own definitions (_KNOWN_ACTIONS, _GENERIC_DISPATCH_HANDLERS, emit_generic, the locks at audit_emit.py:483-484). Every name resolves. The _OPTIMIZER_ALLOWLISTS keys used in group 01 exist in boot_schemas.py:206-230. There is no latent NameError or KeyError on a dispatch path.\n- No duplicate definitions: none of the constants defined in this payload's files is also defined in another part, so declarations.update() cannot silently shadow one.\n- Rebinding covers every function: the parts contain no decorators, classes or lambdas, so nothing escapes install() with template globals.\n- Parts import on their own: module-level code uses only re (imported in harness_schemas.py:7 and lifecycle_schemas.py:7) and two relative imports (harness_schemas.py:8 and optimizer.py:7), and those resolve to tuples or frozensets. Annotations such as Dict, Any, List and Optional sit under `from __future__ import annotations` or on locals, so they are never evaluated.\n- Copying is safe: deepcopy of the frozensets, dicts and compiled patterns is valid on Python 3.7+ (re.Pattern deepcopy returns self), and no Lock or module object appears in any __all__.\n- Dispatch is complete: 173 `if action ==/in` branches, 173 `state[\"event\"] = event` lines and 173 `return True`, so no branch can write the unscrubbed event. The only three bare returns are the intended closed-enum drops: persona_coverage_synthesized, tier_policy_loader_fallback_observed and audit_producer_path_pollution_detected. _dispatch_generic treats None as drop, True as write and False as try the next group. No action name has a branch in two groups. The passthrough check is last (generic_dispatch_05.py:352), and default-deny still writes {\"action\"} only (generic_dispatch_05.py:375-377).\n- The ghost-action guard follows the new layout: test_audit_emit_ghost_action_guard.py:59-76 parses _GENERIC_DISPATCH_HANDLERS.\n- Python 3.9 and stdlib-only: met.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS THIS PAYLOAD TOUCHES\n- Condition 5: true. check_arbitration_kernel.py:312 inscribes \".claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/**/*\", which covers every file in this payload.\n- Condition 7: true. Logic moved to modules, the facade keeps the path, and a hand patch to the old monolith would have to be redone in the part.\n- Condition 6: consistent here. lifecycle_schemas.py:508 adds the \"bash_indirect\" rail and the CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD and CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS switches, which match the producer at bash_indirect_telemetry.py:59-60. A disabled-rail event is therefore not coerced to \"observe\" or \"other\".\n- No other condition is affected by these files. I found no inversion of fail direction, no write outside its place and no partial write in these files: they only declare data and call _write_event.\n\nP0: none.\nP1: none.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): none.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\nP2-1. _lib/audit_emit.py:473-480 (the facade, which ships in another payload but is this code's only entry point). emit_generic carries two do-nothing branches, `if action == \"tool_call_lifecycle_recorded\"` and `if action == \"settings_tamper_detected\"`. Both just call _dispatch_generic. They keep test_ceo_boot_tamper_tripwires.py:525 green, because that test greps inspect.getsource(emit_generic) for the branch. The real scrub gates are generic_dispatch_04.py:62 and :159. Scenario: delete the real settings_tamper_detected branch and that source assertion still passes. The ghost-action guard would still go red, so the cover is thinner, not gone. Minimal fix: point the assertion at the sources in _GENERIC_DISPATCH_HANDLERS, as the ghost-action guard does, and delete the stub branches.\n\nP2-2. The part modules only work through install(). Example: harness_schemas.py:12-14 _learning_token_field uses _LEARNING_ID_RE, which lives in lifecycle_schemas and is never imported. Every emitter in harness.py, lifecycle.py and operations.py likewise uses _write_event and _scrub_ceo_boot_event, which they don't define. Scenario: a future caller does `from _lib._audit_emit_parts.harness import emit_codex_tool_recorded` and gets a NameError at call time instead of a fail-open emit. Today the only importers are install() (audit_emit.py:413, check_agent_spawn.py:128, scripts/lessons.py:204) and audit_emit.py:21. The first iter_events (audit_emit.py:382) delegates to the unbound replay template, but it is replaced before any caller can reach it. Minimal fix: a test that rejects any import of a `_lib._audit_emit_parts.<part>` outside the facade binders, and drop the line-21 import.\n\nP2-3. The failure unit grew. `import _lib.audit_emit` now needs all 33 parts plus a deepcopy of every declaration at each hook start, so one missing or unreadable part (an interrupted copy or a partial upgrade) breaks every emitter at once. Before, the failure unit was one file. install.sh:1522-1541 copies `_lib/*` entry by entry with cp -R. I could not check upgrade.sh: it has no redacted copy in this tree (no scripts/upgrade.sh), presumably among the withheld files. Through _python-hook.sh a crash with no decision fails open, which fits the infra-fault rule. For the fail-CLOSED canonical-edit wall (ADR-186), the effect depends on that hook's own import guard, which is in another payload. Minimal fix: an install and upgrade smoke step that imports _lib.audit_emit in the target tree after the copy.\n\nP2-4. generic_dispatch_04.py:266. The redactor replaced the clamp ceiling of compaction_continuity_snapshot.chain_length with [REDACTED:rg], so this payload cannot show whether the value equals the base. This is not a defect claim. Minimal fix: the maintainer confirms the literal against the v1.4.3-rc.1 monolith.\n\nP2-5. The dispatch bodies were regenerated mechanically. Signs: redundant parentheses such as `(_hs > 599)` and `\"\".join((c for c ...))`, and a leftover `pass` at generic_dispatch_05.py:353. The per-branch security-rationale comments of the old emit_generic did not survive into the groups. Behavior is unaffected. Minimal fix (optional): restore the rationale comments or point to them in ADR-202.","decision":"GO","sentence":"The thirteen new audit_emit part modules are a complete, consistently rebound split: every free name resolves through install() into the facade, all 173 dispatch branches scrub and set the event before writing, the closed-enum drops and default-deny are preserved, and the conditions this payload touches (5, 7 and the condition-6 enum) hold against the code, so it leaves no false condition and no P0, only P2 follow-ups."},"ttft_ms":10807,"type":"result","duration_ms":508361,"uuid":"88cf3281-b3aa-4a15-8a7b-281f4dc8c2c7","ttft_stream_ms":4072,"time_to_request_ms":39,"first_content_frame_ms":4073,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":62,"outputTokens":50911,"cacheReadInputTokens":5181986,"cacheCreationInputTokens":225253,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.856889199999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":36475,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":225253,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-20T15:54:23.997Z"}
